['{
  "question": "Explain why multiplying a decimal number by 10 moves the decimal point one place to the right. Use an example to illustrate your explanation and discuss how this concept applies to real-world measurements, such as converting meters to centimeters.",
  "answer": "When you multiply a decimal number by 10, the decimal point moves one place to the right because each digit in the number shifts to a place value that is ten times larger. For example, if you have the number 3.45 and multiply it by 10, you get 34.5. The digit 3, which was in the ones place, moves to the tens place, the digit 4, which was in the tenths place, moves to the ones place, and the digit 5, which was in the hundredths place, moves to the tenths place. This concept applies to real-world measurements like converting meters to centimeters. Since 1 meter equals 100 centimeters, multiplying a length in meters by 100 moves the decimal point two places to the right, effectively converting meters to centimeters. For instance, 2.5 meters becomes 250 centimeters when multiplied by 100.",
  "reasoning": "The question requires students to explain a mathematical concept and apply it to a real-world context, which aligns with the target cognitive demand of understanding decimals at a DOK4 level. The explanation involves comprehension of decimal place value and its application to measurement conversion, ensuring a deep understanding of the concept."
}', '{"question": "Explain why multiplying a decimal number by 10 moves the decimal point one place to the right. Use an example to illustrate your explanation and discuss how this concept applies to real-world measurements, such as converting meters to centimeters.", "answer": "When you multiply a decimal number by 10, the decimal point moves one place to the right because each digit in the number shifts to a place value that is ten times larger. For example, if you have the number 3.45 and multiply it by 10, you get 34.5. The digit 3, which was in the ones place, moves to the tens place, the digit 4, which was in the tenths place, moves to the ones place, and the digit 5, which was in the hundredths place, moves to the tenths place. This concept applies to real-world measurements like converting meters to centimeters. Since 1 meter equals 100 centimeters, multiplying a length in meters by 100 moves the decimal point two places to the right, effectively converting meters to centimeters. For instance, 2.5 meters becomes 250 centimeters when multiplied by 100.", "reasoning": "The question requires students to explain a mathematical concept and apply it to a real-world context, which aligns with the target cognitive demand of understanding decimals at a DOK4 level. The explanation involves comprehension of decimal place value and its application to measurement conversion, ensuring a deep understanding of the concept."}']

