['{
  "question": "A student has two containers. Container A holds 100 ml of a solution with 20% salt by volume, and Container B holds 150 ml of a solution with 30% salt by volume. After mixing the contents of both containers, the student observes that the resulting mixture has a certain percentage of salt by volume. Explain why the concentration of salt in the final mixture is not simply the average of the two initial concentrations, and describe how the volume of each solution affects the final concentration. Provide a real-world example where this concept might be applied.",
  "answer": "The concentration of salt in the final mixture is not simply the average of the two initial concentrations because the volumes of the solutions are different. When mixing solutions, the final concentration depends on the total amount of solute (salt) and the total volume of the mixture. In this case, Container A has 20 ml of salt (20% of 100 ml) and Container B has 45 ml of salt (30% of 150 ml). The total salt is 65 ml in a total volume of 250 ml, resulting in a concentration of 26%. This is not the average of 20% and 30% (which would be 25%). The volume of each solution affects the final concentration because a larger volume contributes more to the total volume and total solute. A real-world example is preparing a saline solution for medical use, where precise concentrations are needed based on the volumes of the components mixed.",
  "reasoning": "The question requires understanding the concept of mixtures and solutions, specifically how concentrations change when solutions are combined. It involves explaining why the final concentration is not a simple average and how the volume of each solution impacts the result. This aligns with Bloom's 'Understand' level as it requires comprehension rather than just recall or application. The DOK4 rating reflects the complexity of analyzing and explaining the concept in a real-world context."
