['{
  "question": "Predict the product of an SN2 reaction between a nucleophile and a substrate with a good leaving group under polar protic and polar aprotic solvent conditions, and explain how the solvent polarity affects the reaction rate, including the role of solvation and transition state stabilization.",
  "answer": "In an SN2 reaction, the nucleophile attacks the electrophilic carbon center from the opposite side of the leaving group, resulting in inversion of stereochemistry. The product is determined by the nucleophile replacing the leaving group. Under polar protic conditions, the solvent stabilizes the nucleophile through hydrogen bonding, reducing its nucleophilicity and slowing the reaction. In polar aprotic solvents, the nucleophile is less solvated, making it more reactive and increasing the reaction rate. Solvation of the transition state also plays a crucial role; in polar aprotic solvents, the transition state is stabilized due to better charge dispersion, further enhancing the reaction rate.",
  "reasoning": "The question requires predicting the product of an SN2 reaction, which involves applying knowledge of reaction mechanisms. It also requires explaining the effect of solvent polarity on reaction rate, which involves analyzing the role of solvation and transition state stabilization. This aligns with the Apply level of Bloom's taxonomy and DOK4, as it requires integrating and applying complex concepts to predict outcomes and explain mechanisms."
}', '{"question": "Predict the product of an SN2 reaction between a nucleophile and a substrate with a good leaving group under polar protic and polar aprotic solvent conditions, and explain how the solvent polarity affects the reaction rate, including the role of solvation and transition state stabilization.", "answer": "In an SN2 reaction, the nucleophile attacks the electrophilic carbon center from the opposite side of the leaving group, resulting in inversion of stereochemistry. The product is determined by the nucleophile replacing the leaving group. Under polar protic conditions, the solvent stabilizes the nucleophile through hydrogen bonding, reducing its nucleophilicity and slowing the reaction. In polar aprotic solvents, the nucleophile is less solvated, making it more reactive and increasing the reaction rate. Solvation of the transition state also plays a crucial role; in polar aprotic solvents, the transition state is stabilized due to better charge dispersion, further enhancing the reaction rate.", "reasoning": "The question requires predicting the product of an SN2 reaction, which involves applying knowledge of reaction mechanisms. It also requires explaining the effect of solvent polarity on reaction rate, which involves analyzing the role of solvation and transition state stabilization. This aligns with the Apply level of Bloom's taxonomy and DOK4, as it requires integrating and applying complex concepts to predict outcomes and explain mechanisms."}']

